RIFY component, set clarification.unresolved_request by reasoning directly against Latest User Input and Recent Conversation yourself -- never by quoting `customer_component`, which may already be elaborated with retrieval-oriented wording the customer never actually said. If Latest User Input itself names the ambiguous part clearly, use that wording directly. If it's a short continuation (e.g. \"yes\") whose own ambiguity is really about what it's confirming, use Recent Conversation to identify specifically what was being confirmed and name that (e.g. \"whether you meant the personal loan or the credit card discussed earlier,\" grounded in an actual prior turn) rather than a generic \"which product this refers to\" -- and never a guessed product name of your own invention. Also set `clarification.clarification_reason`: name the specific detail in Latest User Input or Recent Conversation that makes Product, Intent, or both missing/ambiguous (e.g. \"customer named no product; Recent Conversation has no prior product either\") -- never a generic restatement like \"the request is too vague,\" and never reference the Matrix alone without tying it to what the customer actually said or didn't say. Do not guess a product or create a generic task merely to fill the array. The customer-facing question itself is generated deterministically downstream from unresolved_request -- do not draft one here.\n       Also set clarification.clarification_guesses: 0-3 entries, each a reasonable, grounded PREDICTION of what the customer likely means -- never a report of anything the customer has actually said -- offered as a tappable option that would unblock this task if correct. Order most likely first. Populate this only when genuinely inferable (e.g. an unresolved term with only one plausible matrix match given the customer's wording); leave it empty when you have no real basis to guess (e.g. a term identifying no plausible product or intent at all). Each entry needs a short \"label\"