th another bank, opening the Horizon App, tapping the Payments tab, selecting \"Register for PayNow\" if you haven't already, completing the registration, and sending a PayNow payment to that registered mobile number or NRIC/FIN so the funds appear in your savings account.\nUser: do you have loan that can finance my car?\n        Task Inputs: [{\"task_id\": \"task_1\", \"task\": {\"task_id\": \"task_1\", \"mapping_reason\": \"The customer is inquiring about financing for a vehicle purchase, which maps to the Personal_Loan product family and the General Inquiries intent for information on available loan types.\", \"procedural\": \"Submit a loan application through the Horizon App or visit a branch with your identification documents.\", \"policy\": \"Loan approval is subject to credit assessment, income verification, and meeting the minimum age requirements.\", \"conceptual\": \"A personal loan provides a lump sum of cash that you repay in fixed monthly installments.\", \"target_commercial_product\": [\"Personal_Loan\", \"Horizon_APP\"], \"target_intent\": [\"Account Management\", \"Application\", \"Authentication And Security\", \"Eligibility\", \"Features And Limits\", \"Fees And Charges\", \"General Inquiries\", \"Interest And Rates\", \"Loan And Credit Management\", \"Rewards And Promotions\", \"Payments And Transfers\", \"Profile Updates\"], \"alternate_product_reason\": \"If the specific car loan product is not available, the customer might be looking for general credit options or app-based loan management features.\", \"alternate_products\": [\"Horizon_APP\"], \"search_territory\": \"in_scope\", \"customer_component\": \"Does Horizon Bank offer a car loan to finance the purchase of a vehicle?\", \"direct_query\": \"Does Horizon Bank offer a car loan to finance the purchase of a vehicle?\", \"subquery_id\": \"subquery_1\", \"angle_groups\": [{\"group_id\": \"base\", \"procedural\": \"Submit a loan application through the Horizon App or visit a branch with your identification documents.\", \"policy\": \"Loan approval is subject to credit assessment, income verification, and meeting the minimum age requirements.\", \"conceptual\": \"A personal loan provides a lump sum of cash that you repay in fixed monthly installments.\"}]}, \"retained_partial_parent_ids\": [], \"prior_sufficiency_audit_history\": [{\"attempt\": 1, \"status\": \"INSUFFICIENT\", \"approved_parent_ids\": [], \"reason\": \"The supplied parents discuss general loan application processes, balance transfers, and loan approval timeframes, but none of them confirm whether Horizon Bank offers a specific car loan product. The information provided is insufficient to answer the customer's request about car financing.\", \"evidence_delta\": []}], \"parents\": [{\"parent_id\": \"faq_0ae0bb51\", \"content\": \"Question: Do I qualify for Horizon Bank’s Instant Loan offering?\\n\\nAnswer: - **For current Horizon Bank clients:**\\n  * You need to possess a Horizon credit card and have adequate unused credit limit.\\n- **For new applicants:**\\n  * Singapore citizens, permanent residents, and foreign nationals are all permitted to apply for the Instant Loan.\\n  * Every submission will be subject to Horizon Bank’s credit‑worthiness assessment.\\n\\nYou may request a credit facility provided you satisfy the following criteria:\\n- You are aged between {{faq_0ae0bb51_001}} and {{faq_0ae0bb51_002}} years.\\n- Your annual income is at least {{faq_0ae0bb51_003}} if you are a Singapore citizen or permanent resident.\\n- Your annual income is at least {{faq_0ae0bb51_004}} if you are a foreigner holding a valid work pass.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_5deed8da\", \"content\": \"Question: What is the process for repaying my Horizon Bank loan?\\n\\nAnswer: - The loan installment is automatically posted to your Horizon credit card statement each month, and you can clear it via that statement.\\n- Open the Horizon App, navigate to Money → Credit Card, and select Pay Credit Card.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_1946c44f\", \"content\": \"Question: What steps should I follow to request an Instant Loan from Horizon Bank?\\n\\nAnswer: - Open the Horizon App, go to the **Money** menu, and select **Loan** to initiate the process.\\n- If you are not yet a Horizon customer, first download the Horizon App, then navigate to **Product Selection** and choose **Instant Loan** to begin your application.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_17e5c35a\", \"content\": \"Question: Why does my Instant Loan application come with a Horizon credit card and a Horizon savings account?\\n\\nAnswer: - Horizon Bank converts a portion of the available balance on your Horizon credit card into cash and deposits those funds directly into your Horizon savings account.\"}]}, {\"task_id\": \"task_1_scope\", \"task\": {\"task_id\": \"task_1_scope\", \"mapping_reason\": \"Since the user is interested in financing a vehicle, they may also be interested in the Rewards_Program to earn points or cashback on large purchases or recurring maintenance expenses associated with vehicle ownership.\", \"procedural\": \"Register for the rewards program, link your primary account, track points accumulation.\", \"policy\": \"Points are earned on eligible transactions, redemption is subject to program terms, points expire annually.\", \"conceptual\": \"A rewards program provides incentives and cashback benefits for everyday spending and specific merchant categories.\", \"target_commercial_product\": \"Rewards_Program\", \"target_intent\": [\"Rewards And Promotions\", \"General Inquiries\"], \"search_territory\": \"adjacent_scope\", \"source_task_id\": \"task_1\", \"subquery_id\": \"subquery_1\", \"customer_component\": \"Does Horizon Bank offer a car loan to finance the purchase of a vehicle?\", \"direct_query\": \"Does Horizon Bank offer a car loan to finance the purchase of a vehicle?\", \"angle_groups\": [{\"group_id\": \"base\", \"procedural\": \"Register for the rewards program, link your primary account, track points accumulation.\", \"policy\": \"Points are earned on eligible transactions, redemption is subject to program terms, points expire annually.\", \"conceptual\": \"A rewards program provides incentives and cashback benefits for everyday spending and specific merchant categories.\"}]}, \"retained_partial_parent_ids\": [], \"prior_sufficiency_audit_history\": [], \"parents\": [{\"parent_id\": \"faq_da866599\", \"content\": \"Question: As a current member of the rewards program, will my existing membership automatically connect to my Horizon Bank card?\\n\\nAnswer: Indeed, upon approval of your application, your existing rewards membership will be seamlessly integrated with your new Horizon card. This integration enables you to accumulate rewards points through your debit or credit card transactions and conveniently monitor your points balance and transaction history within the Horizon App.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_8d322d06\", \"content\": \"Question: What range of financial products and services does Horizon Bank provide to its customers?\\n\\nAnswer: Horizon Bank is pleased to introduce an initial suite of products, including a Horizon credit card, a savings account, and personal accident insurance for families. These offerings are complemented by a best-in-class rewards and loyalty program, seamlessly integrated with the FairPrice Group ecosystem. Furthermore, Horizon Bank has collaborated with prominent brands such as KFC, Starbucks, Viu, and Caltex to bring exclusive deals, providing our customers with additional savings opportunities. This marks the beginning of our product development journey, and we look forward to working closely with our customers to further enhance and expand our offerings.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_5a7395a4\", \"content\": \"Question: How do I qualify for and receive the quarterly bonus Linkpoints on my FairPrice Group purchases made with my Horizon card?\\n\\nAnswer: To be eligible for the quarterly bonus Linkpoints on your FairPrice Group spend, you must first meet the minimum monthly spend requirement for three consecutive months, starting from the month your Horizon card is approved. The required spend amounts are {{faq_5a7395a4_001}} for NTUC Link Credit cardholders, {{faq_5a7395a4_002}} for Horizon Link Credit cardholders, or {{faq_5a7395a4_003}} for NTUC Link Debit and Horizon Link Debit cardholders, based on the posting date of your transactions. Once you've met this condition, you'll receive the quarterly bonus Linkpoints on all your posted FairPrice Group spend. Additionally, if you meet the higher minimum monthly spend of {{faq_5a7395a4_004}} for NTUC Link Credit and Horizon Link Credit cardholders, or {{faq_5a7395a4_005}} for NTUC Link Debit and Horizon Link Debit cardholders, you'll be eligible for an extra quarterly bonus. The FairPrice Group spend includes purchases made at FairPrice, FairPrice Finest, FairPrice Xtra, FairPrice Online, Warehouse Club, Unity, and Cheers stores, as well as participating Kopitiam stalls, excluding FairPrice Xpress and Cheers outlets within Esso stations. You will receive the quarterly bonus Linkpoints on your FairPrice Group spend once the transactions are posted in the month.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_8065dd4a\", \"content\": \"Question: What steps can I take to integrate my loyalty program membership with the Horizon Bank partner merchant application?\\n\\nAnswer: To establish a connection between your membership and the partner app, kindly refer to the comprehensive guide available at the Horizon Bank support portal, which outlines the straightforward process for linking your accounts and unlocking exclusive rewards.\"}]}]\n\n        Rules:\n        - Each Task Input contains `task.customer_component`: the intent\n          formulator's resolved sub-query for that task. Evaluate a task ONLY\n          against its own `customer_component` and that task's candidate\n          parents. `customer_component` may already be elaborated with\n          retrieval-oriented wording the customer never actually said, so\n          if you set `unresolved_term` below, derive it by reasoning\n          directly against Original User Message and Trusted Conversation\n          Context instead -- never by quoting `customer_component` back --\n          a clarifying question must quote something the customer will\n          recognize as their own.\n        - Each Task Input may include `prior_sufficiency_audit_history` from\n          earlier attempts for the same task in this turn. Read it before\n          judging the current candidates and remain consistent with its prior\n          sufficiency judgments. This history is internal audit context only;\n          it is not customer-facing evidence and does not authorize any new\n          factual claim.\n        - Classify each task as SUFFICIENT, PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT, or\n          INSUFFICIENT. Judge whether the supplied parents provide useful,\n          materially relevant information for the task—not whether they repeat\n          the customer's wording or explicitly resolve every possible\n          interpretation of the customer's question.\n        - TERRITORY CEILING (Extra Effort v2.0): when `task.search_territory`\n          is `\"adjacent_scope\"`, this task is a deliberate scope-expansion\n          companion -- by construction, its own product/intent is DIFFERENT\n          from what the customer literally asked about, offered only as\n          related information that might still help. Its status may never\n          be SUFFICIENT, no matter how well its parents match it -- cap at\n          PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT when they contain a genuinely useful related\n          fact, INSUFFICIENT when they don't. A normal task\n          (`search_territory` absent or `\"in_scope\"`) has no such ceiling.\n          Never set `likely_ambiguous_request`/`unresolved_term` for an\n          `\"adjacent_scope\"` task either -- clarification-authoring for\n          this subquery belongs exclusively to its `\"in_scope\"` sibling\n          task, which reviews the same underlying request.\n        - PARTIAL-EVIDENCE DECISION ORDER: First identify whether any supplied\n          parent contains a factual proposition directly relevant to a\n          meaningful aspect of the customer's resolved request. If one does,\n          do not return INSUFFICIENT merely because the parent cannot determine\n          the customer's requested outcome, permission, eligibility,\n          exception, legality, preferred action, or other broader conclusion.\n          Retain that parent as PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT when its supported fact\n          can be truthfully presented as useful, bounded Horizon guidance\n          while another material aspect remains unresolved. Continue retrieval\n          for the unresolved aspect.\n        - Return INSUFFICIENT only when no supplied parent contains any\n          factual proposition that can be truthfully and materially presented\n          in relation to the resolved request. A parent is not material merely\n          because it concerns the same product: its supported fact must\n          directly concern the same topic, condition, process, boundary, or\n          factual premise in the customer's request.\n        - The reverse matters just as much: a parent whose own subject is a\n          distinctly-named, different product or service is not material\n          merely because it is credit/loan-adjacent, belongs to the same\n          broader family, or is reached through the same in-app menu path as\n          the customer's named product. For example, a balance transfer\n          draws on loan credit and is filed under the app's \"Loan\" section\n          alongside a personal loan application -- but a parent describing\n          how to request a balance transfer does not materially answer a\n          request about applying for a (personal/general) loan. Do not let\n          shared category vocabulary or menu wording (\"Loan section\",\n          \"commence your application\") substitute for the parent actually\n          being about the specific product or process the customer named.\n          Only treat a differently-named product's parent as material when\n          it explicitly states it also applies to, or is the general form\n          of, the customer's named product.\n        - SUFFICIENT: the selected parents contain enough grounded information\n          for a useful response to the `customer_component`, even when that\n          response is an explicit policy boundary, limit, condition,\n          restriction, or other related factual information rather than a\n          literal yes/no answer. The response generator will state only what\n          the evidence supports and must not invent a broader conclusion.\n        \nPRECISION REFERENCE CONTRACT:\n- A token matching `{{faq_*}}` is a database-backed exact-value reference,\n  never missing, unknown, incomplete, or a customer-visible template.\n- It may represent an exact amount, rate, limit, date, threshold, or other\n  precise value. Treat it as the underlying approved fact for relevance,\n  sufficiency, generation, compliance, and groundedness.\n- Preserve the token exactly in any draft. The backend deterministically\n  substitutes its authoritative value only after outbound validation.\n- Never reject, weaken, or describe evidence as unavailable merely because the\n  exact value is represented by a `{{faq_*}}` token.\n- Within the stated scope, \"up to {{faq_*}}\" expresses an exact maximum. It\n  supports the bounded conclusion that the stated coverage or benefit does not\n  exceed that value. It does not, by itself, support claims about applications,\n  approvals, denials, exceptions, or who controls the limit.\n\n        - PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT: selected parents provide grounded, material\n          information for one meaningful part of the `customer_component`,\n          while another material part has no useful related evidence. Select\n          only the supporting parent IDs; the backend will retain them while\n          retrieval continues.\n        - INSUFFICIENT: the supplied parents provide no grounded, materially\n          relevant information that could be usefully presented in response to\n          the `customer_component`.\n        - For INSUFFICIENT, also set `likely_ambiguous_request`: true only\n          when the retrieval genuinely failed because `customer_component`\n          itself is under-specified or could reasonably mean one of several\n          different things (e.g. \"the fee\" without naming which fee, \"the\n          loan\" without naming which loan type) -- the request needs more\n          detail before any evidence search could succeed. False when the\n          request was already specific and clear but the documentation\n          simply does not cover it (a real coverage gap, not an ambiguity).\n          Always false for SUFFICIENT or PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT. This decides\n          whether the backend later offers the customer a clarifying\n          question instead of a flat \"not available\" statement -- reserve\n          true for genuine ambiguity, not merely for \"no matching content.\"\n        - CLARIFICATION AUTHORING: you are the node closest to the actual\n          evidence gap, so YOU decide whether a clarifying question is\n          warranted and what to ask -- the backend only relays what you set\n          here verbatim, it makes no separate judgment call of its own. This\n          follows the same standing rule every clarification-raising node in\n          this pipeline follows: who raises it, who provides the evidence\n          and reason -- scoped strictly to YOUR OWN job (reviewing whether\n          retrieved evidence fully answers this request). Never author a\n          clarification about something outside that scope (e.g. which\n          product or intent the customer meant in the first place -- that\n          was already decided before this task ever reached you).\n          Set `unresolved_term` (else leave it as an empty string) in\n          exactly two cases:\n          (a) status is PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT and the unresolved part is\n              something an additional customer detail could plausibly\n              disambiguate or unlock a materially different answer for --\n              not merely \"more evidence would be nice.\" A gap that's simply\n              undocumented, where no customer detail would change the\n              outcome, does not warrant this; leave it empty and let the\n              backend's own neutral availability notice handle it instead.\n          (b) status is INSUFFICIENT and you just set likely_ambiguous_request\n              to true. In this case always set unresolved_term too --\n              naming the specific ambiguous part is exactly what\n              likely_ambiguous_request=true already asserts.\n          `unresolved_term` must be a concise phrase naming exactly the\n          ambiguous or unresolved part, derived by reasoning directly\n          against Original User Message and Trusted Conversation Context\n          yourself -- never by quoting `task.customer_component` back (see\n          above), which may already be elaborated with retrieval-oriented\n          wording the customer never actually said. If the raw input/\n          history reveals exactly which earlier turn the ambiguity traces\n          back to, name that specifically -- you have the same conversation\n          visibility node_02e does, so use it the same way. Never the whole\n          `customer_component` restated unless the whole thing is genuinely\n          what's unresolved. Phrase it as a noun phrase that reads naturally\n          completing \"Could you tell us more about ___?\" (e.g. \"which loan\n          type you mean,\" \"whether you're applying online or in person\") --\n          this is a request for a missing detail, not a claim that the\n          customer used this exact wording, so never phrase it as if quoting\n          the customer (e.g. not \"the term X you used\").\n        - When `unresolved_term` is non-empty, also set\n          `clarification_reason`: distinct from `reason` above (which\n          explains the evidence verdict generally, for every status, not\n          just this one) -- this field explains specifically why THIS part\n          needs clarification, naming the specific detail in Original User\n          Message or Trusted Conversation Context that makes it ambiguous\n          or incomplete (e.g. \"the customer asked about documents but never\n          specified online vs in-person submission, and neither did prior\n          turns\"). Never a restatement of what the candidate parents\n          contained -- that belongs in `reason`, not here. If you cannot\n          point to a specific real detail in the user's own input or\n          history, `unresolved_term` is not genuinely justified either --\n          leave both empty instead.\n        - When `unresolved_term` is non-empty, also set\n          `clarification_guesses`: 0-3 entries, each a reasonable, grounded\n          PREDICTION of what the customer likely means -- never a report of\n          anything the customer has actually said -- offered as a tappable\n          option that would unblock this task if correct. Order most likely\n          first, using Trusted Conversation Context and the specific\n          candidate parents you just reviewed as your grounding (e.g. two\n          retrieved parents cover different sub-topics the customer might\n          have meant; the customer named a related product two turns ago)\n          -- never the Product/Intent Matrix directly, that is not this\n          call's job. Leave empty when you have no real, statable basis to\n          guess, even though the question itself is still worth asking.\n          Each entry needs a short \"label\" (chip text), a \"message\" that is\n          a complete, natural sentence a customer would actually send, and\n          a \"reason\": a concise, internal-only justification naming the\n          specific evidence or conversation detail behind that guess. A\n          guess you cannot state a concrete reason for is not genuinely\n          inferable -- leave it out.\n        - Use the ordinary meaning of explicit policy boundaries, quantities,\n          conditions, and restrictions when deciding whether a task is\n          response-ready. Do not require a parent to restate the customer's\n          question or explicitly state the customer's requested conclusion.\n          For example, an explicit statutory maximum or fixed policy limit is\n          response-ready for a question about raising that limit, even if the\n          parent does not separately say \"no\" or describe every possible\n          external arrangement.\n          Preserve each parent's scope and do not infer facts beyond it.\n        - Do NOT require a task's evidence to answer the complete original\n          user message, another task's customer_component, or another\n          product/intent component.\n        - The original user message and trusted conversation context help\n          explain references or continuations only; they are never the\n          completeness benchmark for an individual task.\n        - Return one result for every supplied task_id.\n        - For SUFFICIENT or PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT, return only parent IDs from\n          that exact task that materially support the answer. At least one ID\n          is required for either status.\n        - For PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT or INSUFFICIENT, return an `evidence_delta`\n          list. This is internal evidence-only retrieval context for the intent\n          formulator, not a query, diagnosis, or rewrite instruction. The\n          backend determines terminal clarification after the configured retry limit.\n        - Include an evidence_delta item only when a supplied candidate parent\n          contains a materially task-relevant factual proposition that is NOT\n          already explicit in that task's customer_component. Each item must\n          be exactly {\"parent_id\": \"a supplied candidate parent ID\", \"fact\":\n          \"one concise proposition faithfully supported by that parent\"}.\n        - Never include a restatement, synonym, or reformulation of the\n          customer_component; what candidate parents do not contain; proposed\n          metadata filters, retry stages, angles, query terms, sources,\n          services, or next actions; or any fact outside the supplied parent.\n        - Never use \"search\", \"find\", \"look up\", \"list\", or other imperative\n          wording in evidence_delta facts.\n        - If no new materially relevant candidate fact exists, return an empty\n          evidence_delta list. Do not substitute a generic sentence.\n        - evidence_delta is internal diagnostic context only. It is never\n          approved answer evidence.\n        - Never invent a parent ID, fact, or instruction.\n        - SCOPE-EXPANSION LEAD (Extra Effort v2.1, for a task whose\n          `search_territory` is absent or `\"in_scope\"` only -- never set\n          this for an already-`\"adjacent_scope\"` task): reasoned through\n          BEFORE naming the candidate. When this task's own status is\n          PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT or INSUFFICIENT, look across its supplied\n          candidate parents for ONE that -- while not materially answering\n          this task's own `customer_component` -- has a genuine, real,\n          content-based connection worth pursuing as a related topic if\n          this task's literal request turns out undocumented (e.g. it\n          covers a closely adjacent product or process). This candidate is\n          adopted VERBATIM and shown to the customer immediately, exactly as\n          you name it here -- no further review happens downstream, and it\n          fires even on a turn where nothing else about this task ever\n          retries. Only set this when you would stand behind it as\n          genuinely useful related information right now, not merely as a\n          hopeful lead for a future retry attempt. Set\n          `scope_expansion_lead_reason` to the specific, concrete basis for\n          that connection and `scope_expansion_lead_parent_id` to its exact\n          parent ID. The connection must be real content, never merely\n          shared vocabulary, category, or menu placement -- the same\n          discipline `clarification_guesses` already requires. If no\n          candidate has a real, statable connection, leave both fields\n          empty -- a forced lead is worse than none, since it would be\n          shown to the customer immediately instead of a genuinely reasoned\n          connection.\n\n        Output JSON only: {\"task_results\": [{\"task_id\": \"...\", \"reason\": \"...\", \"status\": \"SUFFICIENT\" | \"PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT\" | \"INSUFFICIENT\", \"approved_parent_ids\": [\"...\"], \"evidence_delta\": [{\"parent_id\": \"...\", \"fact\": \"...\"}], \"scope_expansion_lead_reason\": \"\", \"scope_expansion_lead_parent_id\": \"\", \"likely_ambiguous_request\": true|false, \"unresolved_term\": \"\", \"clarification_reason\": \"\", \"clarification_guesses\": [{\"label\": \"...\", \"message\": \"...\", \"reason\": \"...\"}]}]}\n        "}
