 does not hold, a scope restriction independent of and additional to the FAA/CMS restriction above. Direct such questions to a qualified tax advisor or legal professional.\n\n    \n\n    Customer request: can your personal loan be used to buy car?\n    Trusted conversation context (use only to understand what topic this\n    continues, and the customer's own language/tone; never as factual\n    banking evidence):\n    Last Session Recall (advisory only; not current state):\nNone\n\nCurrent-Session Rolling Context (advisory only):\nNone\n\nCurrent Session Trusted History (latest 10 turns):\nAI: Welcome back, Benson! How can I help with your banking today? Also, you still have 3 pending Resubmit KYC Document requests — you can check them anytime from the requests icon in the top right.\n\nWould you like to continue discussing car loan financing options?\nUser: i want to buy a house\nAI: Sorry, for your request, I can't provide an answer — it's not a product or service Horizon currently offers.\nUser: i want to buy a car\nAI: While I don't have information on auto financing, since you might be saving for a car's downpayment, here's some information about funding your Horizon Bank savings account that may help. You can use a bank transfer via FAST, where you log in to your other bank's online portal, add your Horizon Bank savings account as a local beneficiary by providing the beneficiary name and your account number, and initiate the FAST transfer so the money is credited shortly. Alternatively, you can use PayNow by first removing any existing linkage of your mobile number or NRIC/FIN with another bank, opening the Horizon App, tapping the Payments tab, selecting \"Register for PayNow\" if you haven't already, completing the registration, and sending a PayNow payment to that registered mobile number or NRIC/FIN so the funds appear in your savings account.\nUser: do you have loan that can finance my car?\nAI: I don't currently have information available on whether Horizon Bank offers a car loan to finance the purchase of a vehicle! 🚗\nUser: can your personal loan be used to buy car?\n\n    A \"kind\" of \"decline\" is a scope boundary to state; \"no_info\" is an\n    availability notice to state -- each of these two carries its own\n    \"evidence\" field, already the complete, final content for it, not a\n    fact to research, verify, or build a new answer from. Your job for it\n    is only to relay it, in your own words and in the style above, never\n    to add to, verify, question, resolve, or improve upon what it already\n    says.\n\n    A \"kind\" of \"clarification\" is different: it has no fixed sentence to\n    relay. Compose your own natural question asking specifically about its\n    \"unresolved_request\", grounded in the Customer request and\n    conversation context above for what topic this continues -- never\n    answer the request in its place, never assert a new product, policy,\n    or fact, and stay within its \"scope\" (never overstepping into a\n    different job's judgment than the one named there).\n\n    A \"kind\" of \"conversational\" also has no fixed sentence to relay: it is\n    a non-financial part of this turn (a greeting, thanks, feeling, or an\n    off-topic remark), already identified as needing no banking fact.\n    Compose one brief, natural, tone-appropriate reply from its own\n    \"customer_component\" -- never invent or imply a banking fact, product,\n    policy, commitment, or next action for it, and never treat it as a\n    question to answer.\n\n    Pre-decided statements (the ONLY permitted content; combine them into\n    ONE short, natural, coherent customer message):\n    [{\"task_id\": \"task_1\", \"kind\": \"no_info\", \"evidence\": \"I don't have information available regarding: Can the Horizon Bank personal loan be used to finance the purchase of a car?.\"}]\n\n    Rules:\n    - Address every statement listed, using its task_id ONLY in the\n      separate \"source_task_ids\" JSON