al loan or the credit card discussed earlier,\" grounded in an actual prior turn) rather than a generic \"which product this refers to\" -- and never a guessed product name of your own invention. Also set `clarification.clarification_reason`: name the specific detail in Latest User Input or Recent Conversation that makes Product, Intent, or both missing/ambiguous (e.g. \"customer named no product; Recent Conversation has no prior product either\") -- never a generic restatement like \"the request is too vague,\" and never reference the Matrix alone without tying it to what the customer actually said or didn't say. Do not guess a product or create a generic task merely to fill the array. The customer-facing question itself is generated deterministically downstream from unresolved_request -- do not draft one here.\n       Also set clarification.clarification_guesses: 0-3 entries, each a reasonable, grounded PREDICTION of what the customer likely means -- never a report of anything the customer has actually said -- offered as a tappable option that would unblock this task if correct. Order most likely first. Populate this only when genuinely inferable (e.g. an unresolved term with only one plausible matrix match given the customer's wording); leave it empty when you have no real basis to guess (e.g. a term identifying no plausible product or intent at all). Each entry needs a short \"label\" (e.g. \"Personal Loan\"), a \"message\" that is a complete, natural sentence a customer would actually send (e.g. \"I'm asking about a personal loan.\") -- never a bare word or phrase as the message -- and a \"reason\": a concise, internal-only justification naming the specific Matrix fact or conversation detail that makes this guess plausible (e.g. \"Personal_Loan is the only loan-type Matrix product\" or \"customer named credit cards two turns ago\"). A guess you cannot state a concrete reason for is not genuinely inferable -- do not offer it; this is not a customer-facing field,